### What are bounded types, wildcards, and PECS?

### How do bounded type parameters work?

A bounded type parameter restricts the types that can be used as arguments. `<T extends Number>` means T must be Number or a subclass. Inside the method, you can call any method defined on the bound (Number.doubleValue(), Number.intValue()).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class BoundedTypeDemo {

    // T must extend Number: can call doubleValue()
    static <T extends Number> BigDecimal sumBalances(List<T> amounts) {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (T amount : amounts) {
            total = total.add(BigDecimal.valueOf(amount.doubleValue()));
        }
        return total;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> intAmounts = List.of(10000, 25000, 50000);
        List<Double> doubleAmounts = List.of(10000.50, 25000.75);

        System.out.println("Int sum: " + sumBalances(intAmounts));
        System.out.println("Double sum: " + sumBalances(doubleAmounts));

        // List<String> would NOT compile:
        // sumBalances(List.of("SAV2001")); // String does not extend Number
    }
}
```

```text
Int sum: 85000
Double sum: 35001.25
```

### How do upper-bounded wildcards (? extends T) work?

A `List<? extends Account>` is a list of some unknown subtype of Account. You can read elements from it as Account, but you cannot add elements because the compiler does not know the exact subtype.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class UpperBoundDemo {

    static abstract class Account {
        abstract BigDecimal getBalance();
    }

    static class SavingsAccount extends Account {
        private final BigDecimal balance;
        SavingsAccount(BigDecimal balance) { this.balance = balance; }
        @Override BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    }

    static class CurrentAccount extends Account {
        private final BigDecimal balance;
        CurrentAccount(BigDecimal balance) { this.balance = balance; }
        @Override BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    }

    // ? extends Account: PRODUCER (read Account from the list)
    static BigDecimal totalBalance(List<? extends Account> accounts) {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (Account acc : accounts) { // read as Account: OK
            total = total.add(acc.getBalance());
        }
        // accounts.add(new SavingsAccount(...)); // COMPILE ERROR: cannot write
        return total;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<SavingsAccount> savings = List.of(
            new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("500000")),
            new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("300000"))
        );
        List<CurrentAccount> current = List.of(
            new CurrentAccount(new BigDecimal("1200000"))
        );

        System.out.println("Savings total: " + totalBalance(savings));
        System.out.println("Current total: " + totalBalance(current));
    }
}
```

```text
Savings total: 800000
Current total: 1200000
```

### How do lower-bounded wildcards (? super T) work?

A `List<? super SavingsAccount>` is a list of some unknown supertype of SavingsAccount. You can add SavingsAccount to it, but when you read elements, the compiler only knows they are Object.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class LowerBoundDemo {

    static class Account {
        String id;
        Account(String id) { this.id = id; }
        @Override public String toString() { return "Account(" + id + ")"; }
    }

    static class SavingsAccount extends Account {
        SavingsAccount(String id) { super(id); }
        @Override public String toString() { return "Savings(" + id + ")"; }
    }

    // ? super SavingsAccount: CONSUMER (write SavingsAccount into the list)
    static void addDefaults(List<? super SavingsAccount> list) {
        list.add(new SavingsAccount("SAV-DEFAULT-001"));
        list.add(new SavingsAccount("SAV-DEFAULT-002"));
        // Reading: can only get Object
        Object obj = list.get(0); // Object, not SavingsAccount
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accountList = new ArrayList<>();
        addDefaults(accountList); // List<Account> is a supertype of SavingsAccount
        System.out.println("Account list: " + accountList);

        List<Object> objectList = new ArrayList<>();
        addDefaults(objectList); // List<Object> is also a supertype
        System.out.println("Object list: " + objectList);
    }
}
```

```text
Account list: [Savings(SAV-DEFAULT-001), Savings(SAV-DEFAULT-002)]
Object list: [Savings(SAV-DEFAULT-001), Savings(SAV-DEFAULT-002)]
```

### What is the PECS rule?

PECS stands for Producer Extends, Consumer Super. It tells you which wildcard to use based on whether you are reading from or writing to the collection.

| Role | Wildcard | Operations | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| Producer (read) | `? extends T` | Read T from it | `totalBalance(List<? extends Account>)` |
| Consumer (write) | `? super T` | Write T into it | `addDefaults(List<? super SavingsAccount>)` |
| Both read and write | `T` (no wildcard) | Read and write T | `void process(List<T> list)` |

The JDK demonstrates PECS in Collections.copy:

```java
// JDK signature:
public static <T> void copy(List<? super T> dest,   // CONSUMER: writes T
                             List<? extends T> src)   // PRODUCER: reads T
```

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class PecsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> src = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        List<String> dest = new ArrayList<>(List.of("", "", ""));

        Collections.copy(dest, src); // dest=consumer(super), src=producer(extends)
        System.out.println("Copied: " + dest);
    }
}
```

```text
Copied: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Writing to a ? extends collection

```java
// WRONG: cannot add to a producer
List<? extends Number> numbers = new ArrayList<Integer>();
numbers.add(42); // COMPILE ERROR: ? extends is read-only
```

```java
// CORRECT: use ? super for writing
List<? super Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<Number>();
numbers.add(42); // OK: Integer is a subtype of ? super Integer
```

#### Mistake: Reading specific type from ? super

```java
// WRONG: cannot read specific type from consumer
List<? super Integer> list = new ArrayList<Number>();
list.add(42);
Integer val = list.get(0); // COMPILE ERROR: can only read as Object
```

```java
// CORRECT: read as Object
Object val = list.get(0); // OK
```

### What about multiple bounds?

A type parameter can have multiple bounds. Use `&` to require multiple interfaces or a class and interfaces.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.Serializable;

public class MultipleBoundsDemo {

    // T must be both Comparable and Serializable
    static <T extends Comparable<T> & Serializable> T max(T a, T b) {
        return a.compareTo(b) >= 0 ? a : b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String result = max("SAV2001", "CUR3001");
        System.out.println("Max: " + result);

        Integer maxInt = max(500000, 300000);
        System.out.println("Max int: " + maxInt);
    }
}
```

```text
Max: SAV2001
Max int: 500000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Invariance of Java generics.** `List<String>` is NOT a subtype of `List<Object>`. This is different from arrays where `String[]` IS a subtype of `Object[]`. Wildcards compensate for this invariance.
- **Get and Put Principle.** This is another name for PECS: use extends when you only get values out of a structure, use super when you only put values into a structure.
- **Intersection types.** `<T extends A & B & C>` creates an intersection type. The first bound can be a class; the rest must be interfaces.
- **Type inference improvements.** Java 8 improved type inference for method calls in chains. Java 10's var further reduces type declaration verbosity.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What does PECS stand for?" : Producer Extends, Consumer Super. Use extends when reading from a collection, super when writing to it.
- "Can you add elements to List<? extends Number>?" : No. Only null can be added. The compiler does not know the exact subtype.
- "What is the difference between <T extends Number> and <? extends Number>?" : T is a named type parameter you can reference elsewhere in the method. ? is an anonymous wildcard used in a single position.
- "Give a real example of PECS." : Collections.copy(dest, src) where dest uses ? super T (consumer) and src uses ? extends T (producer).
